/* =========================================================
   places.js — bhapstar
   ---------------------------------------------------------
   Offline nearest-place lookup, so the sky panel can put a
   name next to a pair of coordinates without asking a
   geocoding service where the reader is. Nothing leaves the
   browser, which is what the copy under the map promises.

   Source: GeoNames via the all-the-cities package, reduced
   to places of 50,000 people or more, deduplicated on a
   half degree grid so a ring of suburbs collapses to the
   city they belong to, then topped up with the largest
   settlement in any four degree cell the filter left empty.
   That last step is what keeps deserts, islands and the
   far north from returning something a thousand miles away.

   Coordinates are stored in hundredths of a degree, which
   is about a kilometre. The lookup is approximate by
   design: it answers "roughly where is this", not "what is
   this address".

   Exposes window.NearestPlace.find(lat, lng) -> null or
     { name, country, km }
   where country is an ISO 3166-1 alpha-2 code. Callers turn
   that into a readable name with Intl.DisplayNames.
   ========================================================= */
